-- CONTROL (write-side narrowing, eta-expanded). Regression row for #2109
-- (the invariance check has no direction to read, so it rejected the SAFE
-- write-side narrowing too), closed by this sprint's slice 4
-- (S-write-narrowing-direction) in its pre-authorized shape (b): the
-- rejection stands, and the diagnostic now names this measured recovery.
--
-- This row pins the RECOVERY the diagnostic advertises. A slot declared to
-- PERMIT `<Stdout>` is initialized with a function that performs FEWER
-- effects. Written point-free (`Ref noop`) that is rejected, because
-- `paramVarianceRow`'s CLOSED~CLOSED arm sees `<Stdout>` vs `<>` and
-- unification is symmetric -- it cannot tell which side is the declaration.
-- Eta-expanded, the lambda's latent row is still an unsolved variable, the
-- CLOSED~CLOSED arm abstains, and ordinary lenient `unifyRow` runs. The
-- acceptance boundary is exactly closed-row-vs-row-variable.
--
-- This MUST STAY ACCEPTED: a diagnostic that names a recovery which does not
-- work is worse than no advice at all. Its REJECT twin is
-- write-launder-reject, which puts the UNSAFE direction at the same argument
-- site -- the pair exists so a future regression cannot silently swap the two
-- verdicts on this one shape.
noop : String -> Unit
noop s = ()

handler : Ref (String -> <Stdout> Unit)
handler = Ref (s => noop s)

main =
  let _ = handler.value "hi"
  println "ok"
